 "capsule": "In this later phase the name denotes the revived line of Suzdal princes, descended from Andrei Yaroslavich, Alexander Nevsky's brother, who held Suzdal apart from Vladimir town after the Mongol conquest. In 1341 the Golden Horde's Khan Uzbek granted Konstantin Vasilyevich of Suzdal the city of Nizhny Novgorod, and his son Dmitry Konstantinovich briefly held the grand-princely throne of Vladimir itself (1360-1363) in rivalry with Moscow. Khan Tokhtamysh sold the resulting Grand Duchy of Suzdal-Nizhny Novgorod to Vasily I of Moscow in 1392, and although descendants of its princes kept local standing under Muscovite overlordship, the line's remaining claims were extinguished by the later fifteenth century.",
